  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "zend_hash_reverse_apply",
    "file": "php-src/Zend/zend_hash.c",
    "description": "Heap-use-after-free in reverse hash iteration during destructor shutdown: zend_hash_reverse_apply() stores a Bucket* (p = ht->arData + idx), calls apply_func(&p->val), and then may call _zend_hash_del_el(ht, ..., p). The callback path (object destructor) can mutate $GLOBALS and trigger zend_hash_do_resize(), which reallocates ht->arData and frees the old bucket array. After returning, zend_hash_reverse_apply() still uses stale p, and _zend_hash_del_el() dereferences it (p->h / p->key), causing UAF. Crash is observed in _zend_hash_del_el(), but root cause is iterator invalidation/reentrancy-unsafety in zend_hash_reverse_apply().",
    "conditions": "During request shutdown, shutdown_destructors() invokes zend_hash_reverse_apply() on a hashtable (here $GLOBALS symbol table). A destructor callback must reenter and insert enough elements into the same table to trigger zend_hash_do_resize() (reallocation/free of old arData). In this PoV, __destruct() loops writing many new $GLOBALS entries; after callback returns, reverse_apply uses the pre-resize Bucket* and then deletes it, triggering UAF."
  },
